# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: The Third Was Never Printed

**Story & Context:** At 126 BPM, Idris opens with displaced kick, dry rim, clipped snare, and a hat pattern whose strongest accents avoid beat one. Jonah plays only rootless seventh-and-ninth Rhodes shells, deliberately excluding the third. Elena bows a compact fifth-to-second cell whose sympathetic tuning produces a minor third after the direct note begins to decay. That resonance becomes the track’s Hidden Third. Mila enters with a short root-sixth-flat-seventh bass answer after the bloom rather than with the bow attack. In the second section, Idris creates three Resonance Slots by deleting hats exactly where the sympathetic third is strongest. Petar adds one bass-clarinet fall at the end of every fourth phrase but never doubles the gadulka.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close-up of an old tram ticket ledger with one empty column where the fare class should be printed, violet date stamp bleeding into cream paper, gadulka bow hair crossing the foreground, rain reflected in an aluminum desk tray. Original sparse lyric fragments: “the third was never printed / still I heard it / route twelve after rain / leave the column open.”

## Track 2: Rebow at the Yellow Signal

**Story & Context:** At 132 BPM, the EP’s most kinetic track uses Bow Offset as the main hook. Elena begins each bowed note a fraction early, while the sympathetic bloom aligns closer to Idris’s snare and hat grid. The direct attack therefore feels impatient while the resonance feels locked. Mila uses clipped chromatic bass pickups, and Jonah alternates sus2 and dominant-9 shells with frequent empty bars. Dead-Air Rebow appears at the end of every two-bar cell: all melodic instruments leave a sixteenth-note hole between bow directions, letting the re-entry of bow grain function like a percussion hit. Midway through the track, Bruk Hinge rotates one repeated gadulka D from pre-snare pickup to post-kick answer without changing the pitch.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Night tram signal glowing yellow beside wet rails, one route ledger page trapped under a windshield wiper, violet ink streaked by rain, blurred apartment lights beyond. Original sparse lyric fragments: “yellow signal / rebow now / same note, different side / I missed the stop but kept the pulse.”

## Track 3: Sympathy Lines Under Platform Four

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 120 BPM and begins with no snare. Frame drum, sparse kick, bass, and widely spaced gadulka notes define the first sixteen bars. Jonah uses Shell Exchange to voice only fourth and seventh while Mila supplies root and Elena’s sympathetic strings reveal sixth and ninth. The complete chord exists nowhere on a single track. Petar switches from bass clarinet to muted flugelhorn halfway through, holding soft sixths against descending bowed cells. In the final minute, Idris removes kick and hats one by one until only frame-drum fingertip taps remain. Jonah stops playing. Mila holds one tonic. Elena bows one final fifth that excites a major third beneath it; no instrument states that third directly. The EP ends on the sympathetic resonance alone.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Empty tram platform four after rain, violet route lines visible beneath scratched plexiglass, one gadulka case on a metal bench, distant green signal, fluorescent reflections stretching across wet concrete. Original sparse lyric fragments: “platform four / lines underneath / nobody played that note / still it stayed.”

